 "Why shouldn't I grieve for those who die?": {
  "answer": "Kṛṣṇa’s reason is that death does not touch the ātman (Self): it is never born and never dies, so the wise do not mourn either the dead or the living [gita 2.20] [gita 2.11]. Even after bodily destruction, “we all” do not cease to be in ātma-svarūpa (Self-nature) [gita 2.12].\n\n- Śaṅkara says Arjuna’s elders are aśocyāḥ (not to be grieved for) because of good conduct and, ultimately, their eternality [gita 2.11].\n- At the bodily level too, death is certain for the born; regarding what is unavoidable, one should not grieve [gita 2.27].\n- Beings are unmanifest before birth and after death, manifest only in between [gita 2.28].",
