 "Is the world in the mind?": {
  "answer": "Sthiramati’s Yogācāra does not say the world is inside a private mind; it says vijñaptimātra (representation-only) is the mind’s dharmatā (own-nature) marked by non-duality [trimsika 26]. Śaṅkara’s Īśā reading frames it differently: the Self is within the whole world of name, form, and action, and all beings are seen in the Self [isa 5] [isa 6].\n\n- For Sthiramati, “object-presentation” is one of consciousness’s three transformations, alongside maturation and mentation [trimsika 2].\n- The advanced yogic point is the abandonment of grasped/graspers and the mind’s settling in its own nature, not a claim about a physical world located in a head [trimsika 28].",
